1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,478
This is a campaign with nowhere to go.

2
00:00:04,378 --> 00:00:06,552
The crew finished evening two,

3
00:00:07,002 --> 00:00:09,611
and then I tidied my file.

4
00:00:11,411 --> 00:00:15,324
I deleted one step, the one they were on.

5
00:00:16,224 --> 00:00:18,833
And everything after it is hidden,

6
00:00:19,283 --> 00:00:21,891
with nothing left to reveal it.

7
00:00:23,691 --> 00:00:28,474
By the end of this lecture you'll know how that happens,

8
00:00:28,924 --> 00:00:31,098
how to get it back,

9
00:00:31,548 --> 00:00:35,896
and how to never do it to a real crew.

10
00:00:37,096 --> 00:00:40,139
Here's the whole idea, in one sentence.

11
00:00:41,039 --> 00:00:44,083
The save holds what the crew did.

12
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:48,011
Your file holds what there is to do.

13
00:00:49,811 --> 00:00:52,854
The save has no words in it.

14
00:00:53,754 --> 00:00:57,667
It has keys, and a state for each key.

15
00:00:59,467 --> 00:01:02,076
So every time the game starts,

16
00:01:02,526 --> 00:01:05,570
it joins the two halves by key.

17
00:01:06,470 --> 00:01:10,383
Change your words, and the crew sees new words.

18
00:01:11,283 --> 00:01:16,500
Change a key, and the save doesn't know that record any more.

19
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:20,743
We don't practice on a live campaign.

20
00:01:21,643 --> 00:01:23,817
So I copy the folder,

21
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:27,311
and I call the copy revision lab.

22
00:01:29,111 --> 00:01:32,154
Now, the copy has the same title,

23
00:01:32,604 --> 00:01:36,083
and a save is named for its title.

24
00:01:36,983 --> 00:01:40,896
Left alone, the two folders would share one save.

25
00:01:42,696 --> 00:01:47,043
So I add one line to the lab's story file.

26
00:01:47,943 --> 00:01:49,683
Save slot, equals six.

27
00:01:51,483 --> 00:01:56,265
Now the lab keeps its own campaign, in its own file,

28
00:01:56,715 --> 00:01:59,324
and mine is somewhere else entirely.

29
00:02:00,524 --> 00:02:04,002
The lab needs a campaign that's partway through.

30
00:02:04,902 --> 00:02:09,250
So I walk two evenings, as fast as I can.

31
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:13,224
And here's where it stands.

32
00:02:14,124 --> 00:02:15,863
Six steps are done,

33
00:02:16,313 --> 00:02:19,357
and one is open, three of five.

34
00:02:20,257 --> 00:02:22,430
Everything after that is hidden.

35
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:25,489
And there's a job in hand.

36
00:02:27,289 --> 00:02:29,463
Now I close the game,

37
00:02:29,913 --> 00:02:32,087
and I start changing things.

38
00:02:33,287 --> 00:02:35,461
Seven changes, all at once.

39
00:02:37,261 --> 00:02:39,870
I reword the step they're on,

40
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:42,059
and one they've finished.

41
00:02:42,959 --> 00:02:44,698
I add a lead,

42
00:02:45,148 --> 00:02:47,757
and I delete a finished step.

43
00:02:49,557 --> 00:02:52,600
I rename a step the right way,

44
00:02:53,050 --> 00:02:57,398
with a line that says what it was called before.

45
00:02:58,298 --> 00:03:02,646
I rename another the wrong way, with no such line.

46
00:03:03,546 --> 00:03:08,328
And I add a new step behind one that's already done.

47
00:03:10,128 --> 00:03:11,867
Lint has two warnings,

48
00:03:12,317 --> 00:03:15,796
and both are about the step I deleted.

49
00:03:16,696 --> 00:03:19,739
It's telling me I've cut my spine.

50
00:03:21,539 --> 00:03:23,713
Now I continue the game.

51
00:03:24,613 --> 00:03:27,657
There are new words in both places,

52
00:03:28,107 --> 00:03:30,715
and the new lead is there.

53
00:03:31,615 --> 00:03:33,789
The deleted step is gone.

54
00:03:34,689 --> 00:03:38,167
The step I renamed properly is still done.

55
00:03:39,067 --> 00:03:42,111
The one I renamed badly has vanished.

56
00:03:42,561 --> 00:03:47,343
And the new step behind a finished one never shows up.

57
00:03:48,543 --> 00:03:52,457
In round two, I put the deleted step back,

58
00:03:52,907 --> 00:03:55,080
and it comes back done.

59
00:03:55,980 --> 00:03:59,459
The save had kept its state for me.

60
00:04:01,259 --> 00:04:05,172
I tell the hidden step to start at once,

61
00:04:05,622 --> 00:04:07,361
and it's still hidden.

62
00:04:08,261 --> 00:04:12,609
The save remembered it as hidden, and the save won.

63
00:04:14,409 --> 00:04:16,148
And now the accident.

64
00:04:16,598 --> 00:04:20,076
I delete the step the crew is on.

65
00:04:21,876 --> 00:04:24,485
The step after it is stranded.

66
00:04:25,385 --> 00:04:30,167
No next lead, in a campaign somebody has put weeks into.

67
00:04:31,367 --> 00:04:35,280
The game's own guide has a cure for this.

68
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:39,224
I tried it, and it didn't work.

69
00:04:41,024 --> 00:04:44,067
What does work is a new key.

70
00:04:44,967 --> 00:04:49,315
The save decides the state of every key it's seen.

71
00:04:49,765 --> 00:04:54,113
Your file decides the state of a key it hasn't.

72
00:04:55,913 --> 00:05:01,565
So I give the stranded step a key the save has never met,

73
00:05:02,015 --> 00:05:05,493
and I tell it to start at once.

74
00:05:07,293 --> 00:05:11,207
And there it is, back in the quest log.

75
00:05:12,407 --> 00:05:14,146
So here's the list.

76
00:05:15,046 --> 00:05:16,785
Reword anything you like.

77
00:05:17,685 --> 00:05:21,598
Add leads, and add steps ahead of the crew.

78
00:05:22,048 --> 00:05:24,222
That's just writing the campaign.

79
00:05:26,022 --> 00:05:31,239
Rename a key only with the line that says what it was.

80
00:05:32,139 --> 00:05:35,617
Never add a step behind one that's finished.

81
00:05:36,517 --> 00:05:39,126
Never delete the step they're on.

82
00:05:40,926 --> 00:05:43,535
And never, ever change the title.

83
00:05:43,985 --> 00:05:45,724
That's a different campaign.

84
00:05:46,924 --> 00:05:48,663
Now it's your turn.

85
00:05:49,563 --> 00:05:52,607
Make a lab from your own campaign.

86
00:05:53,507 --> 00:05:55,680
Strand a step on purpose,

87
00:05:56,130 --> 00:05:58,739
and bring it back both ways.

88
00:06:00,539 --> 00:06:04,017
Then write three rules on your campaign page,

89
00:06:04,467 --> 00:06:10,120
the three things you'll never do to a file a crew is playing.

90
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:17,137
Next time, we pack the whole thing up and send it out.
